---
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/blog";
import { profile } from "../../data/profile";
const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === "a-force-together")!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BlogLayout
  title={`${post.title}, Lilly Seay`}
  description={post.description}
  image={post.cover}
>
  <article class="post prose-post">
    <header class="post-head wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">
        Blog · My talk at Apple's International Women's Day event, Cupertino,
        March 2024
      </p>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p class="post-meta">
        By {profile.name} · {post.date} · {post.readTime}
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap post-body">
      <figure class="post-figure hero-figure">
        <img src={`${base}/blog/iwd/lilly.jpg`} alt="Lilly Seay" />
      </figure>
      <p class="slide-stat">$2,000,000</p>
      <p>
        Two million dollars. That's the amount of money I've saved utilizing my
        technical skillset over the past 7 years. So how did I do it?
      </p>
      <p>
        Well, my parents were entrepreneurs. I already had the entrepreneurial
        bones, and it wasn’t until I was a teenager that I began connecting with
        software engineers, which ignited a passion inside of me.
      </p>
      <p>
        I decided to study engineering in college. After college I pursued my
        career as a software engineer at a big name company. I did that for
        about a year before deciding to learn iOS Development – which ultimately
        led me down the path of creating my own app and starting a company.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">My superpower</p>
      <p>
        That’s when I realized the superpower I had unleashed: being a technical
        founder.
      </p>
      <h2>Back to the drawing board</h2>
      <p>
        So I quit my job. What was I gonna do with my time? Back to the drawing
        board I went. For months, I daydreamed about developing a location-based
        social media app called Erupt. I couldn't wait to learn how to code this
        iOS app; I had never done app development before.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure two">
        <div>
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/whiteboard-checklist.jpg`}
            alt="Whiteboard checklist of features for the Erupt app, dated Monday, March 6"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/whiteboard-wireframes.jpg`}
            alt="Whiteboard wireframes for Erupt: profile, feed, and camera screens"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The whiteboards from the first months of building Erupt.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Since I had my software engineering experience, I was able to teach
        myself iOS development and start coding Erupt from scratch. It took our
        team three months to code the iOS app and launch on the App Store.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/iwd/first-erupt-post.jpg`}
          alt="The first post on Erupt, launched May 27, 2017"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>The first Erupt post. Launched May 27, 2017.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Starting with no funding, I quickly realized how hard it was going to be
        to market a social media app, and even worse, try to keep up with large
        tech giants like Snapchat and Instagram. Towards the end of my journey
        building Erupt, I started learning how to create animations.
      </p>
      <h2>Design</h2>
      <p>
        I wanted to push our user experience and marketing to the max by
        mastering my design skillset. So I'm spending my days fiddling with
        animation designs and I realized I wanted to create an app that revolves
        around fullscreen animations.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/iwd/designing-animations.jpg`}
          alt="Lilly at her desk designing animations in Photoshop"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </figure>
      <p>
        I rolled that into an idea which had purpose, using my colorful
        animations to create game-like mental health activities.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the time, I was dealing with anxiety and Tappily was not only my
        creative brainchild but my outlet for stress. So in 2017, I couldn't
        wait to dig into Apple's frameworks to figure out how to get my
        animations from the drawing board to an iOS app.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/iwd/tappily-maze.jpg`}
          alt="A Tappily activity screen: a yellow maze with numbered checkpoints leading to a lightbulb"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          One of the game-like mental health activities in Tappily.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="slide-stat">
        You can have an idea and start building, or you can start building and
        have an idea.
      </p>
      <p>
        I came up with Tappily by allowing myself to start building a skill.
        What’s powerful about being a technical founder is you can have an idea
        and start building, or you can start building and have an idea.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">Zero funding</p>
      <p>
        I had an idea, and I started building it, using my superpower as a
        technical founder to push through the obstacles of my apps. I was able
        to start my ideas with zero funding and successfully execute my vision.
      </p>
      <h2>Adapt with change</h2>
      <p>
        Suppose you have a great app, and your users are loving it... And then
        all of the sudden, something happens, let's say a pandemic or what
        sometimes feels like an act of god, and the market is no longer stable.
        What do you do?
      </p>
      <p>
        Let me tell you something... you adapt and go with the change. As a
        technical founder, I had to shift my mindset, ideas, approach many times
        on my journey with Tappily.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">200</p>
      <p>
        It started during beta testing. I had enough features fleshed out to
        release the app on TestFlight. I got over 200 people to beta test
        Tappily. During this time, I received feedback that helped me validate
        the app as I was developing it.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure two">
        <div>
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/beta-feedback-1.jpg`}
            alt="A beta tester message praising the Tappily layout and asking to help spread the word"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/beta-feedback-2.jpg`}
            alt="A message thanking Lilly for building a mental health app"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Messages from Tappily beta testers.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        My favorite part of this experience was to translate my vision at every
        level of my business, seamlessly integrating customer feedback into
        design and development, which quickly turned into App Store updates. I
        was free of the communication bottleneck of a large team.
      </p>
      <h2>Apple Entrepreneur Camp</h2>
      <p>
        Another opportunity which challenged Tappily's value proposition was
        Apple Entrepreneur Camp. During the camp, I got to meet with Apple's
        design and technology evangelists. They gave me detailed and actionable
        feedback that I was able to digest and implement in the same week.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/iwd/entrepreneur-camp-email.jpg`}
          alt="Email from Apple welcoming Lilly to cohort 18 of Apple Entrepreneur Camp for female founders"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Cohort 18 of Apple Entrepreneur Camp, dedicated to female founders.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="slide-stat">Pivot ideas immediately</p>
      <p>
        Pivoting doesn't have to make a big splash in your business; it's more
        like skipping a rock across a pond. Making ripples of progress as each
        attempt is more calculated than the last.
      </p>
      <h2>Sourcing my technical skillset</h2>
      <p>
        Like I mentioned earlier, the pandemic made me shift my mindset
        entirely. I adapted using my technical skills to create a backup income,
        ensuring my business survived an unstable tech market. I did what a lot
        of technical founders do; I started looking for software contracts with
        other startups so I could pay myself to work on Tappily.
      </p>
      <p>
        In a few months, I landed my very first contract to build an iOS app for
        another female founder. I was so excited to establish this income
        source.The coolest part of this venture was that I was getting paid to
        fine tune my app development expertise.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure two">
        <div>
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/contract-work-1.jpg`}
            alt="Lilly working on a laptop at a dining table"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/contract-work-2.jpg`}
            alt="Lilly standing at a desk with the Tappily app on a monitor behind her"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Contract work for other startups paid for the time to build Tappily.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="slide-stat">
        Millions in mistakes learned, without paying the price myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        As a contracted app developer, I got to work inside many startups,
        learning from their cultures, processes, teams, technical decisions, and
        mistakes. I learned millions of dollars worth of technical mistakes,
        without paying the price myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's common for startups with non-technical founders to hire the wrong
        developer, especially in the beginning. I've had the opportunity to help
        founders in this position navigate challenges to get on track. Despite
        my best efforts, these startups still incur losses.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">$50k to $100k</p>
      <p>
        I’ve seen startups lose 50k-100k on the initial development of their MVP
        and some startups paying 10k-20k a month in maintenance costs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Technical founders have a more focused hiring gauge and tend to avoid
        these costs, greatly. We know how to hire an engineer and guide them
        with best practices.
      </p>
      <p>
        These experiences really showed how much I saved my company in
        development costs and technical debt. My best advice is to become an
        expert in your domain so you can hire better, which is something is
        built into the process of being a great technical founder.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">$350k</p>
      <p>
        I decided to estimate how much money I’d saved my company by creating
        Tappily myself. I estimated that the founding team put around 350k worth
        of work into the MVP.
      </p>
      <p>
        And when I migrated Tappily from Storyboard to SwiftUI, I saved the
        company about 75k on the re-design and development. This was the cost
        savings for just one of my apps!
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure two">
        <div>
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/swiftui-migration-1.jpg`}
            alt="Tappily before the SwiftUI migration"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/iwd/swiftui-migration-2.jpg`}
            alt="Tappily after the SwiftUI migration"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Migrating Tappily from Storyboard to SwiftUI.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2>More work. Less time.</h2>
      <p>
        But as technical founder of a startup with no funding, you have to be
        creative enough to bootstrap your app. With limited resources, my
        creativity is put to the test to get more work done in less time. Over
        the years, I've successfully implemented many technical business
        strategies.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">Process automation</p>
      <p>
        The first process I automated was getting users for Tappily. I decided
        to build a bot that would recommend people in my target audience and
        create a custom DM.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">533%</p>
      <p>
        This bot took an 8 hour per day marketing process and cut it down to 1.5
        hours, a 533% improvement in time efficiency, and we were also able to
        increase the downloads by fine tuning the bot.
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">Control over timeline, budget, and quality.</p>
      <p>
        Technical founders have at least one thing on their side: control.
        Through the chaos of wearing many hats, you have control of the
        timeline, budget, and quality of your app products.
      </p>
      <p>
        To sum it up, the founding team saved our company money in many
        categories: design, development, automation, and marketing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Between all of this, we saved around 2 million dollars over 7 years,
      </p>
      <p class="slide-stat">We are a force together.</p>
      <p>
        Through my experience creating many apps, I feel grateful to have
        unleashed my super power of being a technical founder and want to
        support other founders unleashing theirs. Being both technical as well
        as entrepreneurial has truly made me the force I am today.
      </p>

      <section class="post-outro reveal">
        <h2>About this talk</h2>
        <p>
          I gave this talk at "A Force Together: Women in App Development,"
          Apple's International Women's Day event at the Apple Developer Center
          in Cupertino, alongside founders Aurelia Edwards and Bria Sullivan. If
          you're a founder deciding whether to build it yourself, <a
            href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>send me a note</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BlogLayout>
